#### Attention-based approaches

The various attention-based approaches use a \(\phi^{e}\) which is factored into a scalar pairwise-interaction function which returns the unnormalized attention term, denoted \(\alpha^{e}\left(\mathbf{v}_{r_{k}},\mathbf{v}_{s_{k}}\right)=a_{k}^{\prime}\), and a vector-valued non-pairwise term, denoted \(\beta^{e}\left(\mathbf{v}_{s_{k}}\right)=\mathbf{b}_{k}^{\prime}\),

\[\phi^{e}\left(\mathbf{e}_{k},\mathbf{v}_{r_{k}},\mathbf{v}_{s_{k}},\mathbf{u} \right)\coloneqq f^{e}\left(\mathbf{v}_{r_{k}},\mathbf{v}_{s_{k}}\right)= \left(\alpha^{e}\left(\mathbf{v}_{r_{k}},\mathbf{v}_{s_{k}}\right),\,\beta^{e} \left(\mathbf{v}_{s_{k}}\right)\right)=\left(a_{k}^{\prime},\mathbf{b}_{k}^{ \prime}\right)=\mathbf{e}_{k}^{\prime}\]

The single-headed self-attention (SA) in the Transformer architecture (Vaswani et al., 2017), implements the non-local formulation as:

\[\alpha^{e}\left(\mathbf{v}_{r_{k}},\mathbf{v}_{s_{k}}\right) =\exp\left(\text{NN}_{\alpha^{\text{query}}}\left(\mathbf{v}_{r_ {k}}\right)^{\intercal}\cdot\text{NN}_{\alpha^{\text{key}}}\left(\mathbf{v}_ {s_{k}}\right)\right)\] \[\beta^{e}\left(\mathbf{v}_{s_{k}}\right) =\text{NN}_{\beta}\left(\mathbf{v}_{s_{k}}\right)\] \[\phi^{v}\left(\mathbf{\bar{e}}_{i}^{\prime},\mathbf{v}_{i}, \mathbf{u}\right)\coloneqq f^{v}\left(\mathbf{\bar{e}}_{i}^{\prime}\right) =\text{NN}_{v}\left(\mathbf{\bar{e}}_{i}^{\prime}\right)\]

where \(\text{NN}_{\alpha^{\text{query}}}\), \(\text{NN}_{\alpha^{\text{key}}}\), and \(\text{NN}_{\beta}\) are again neural network functions with different parameters and possibly different architectures. They also use a multi-headed version which computes \(N_{h}\) parallel \(\mathbf{\bar{e}}_{i}^{\prime h}\) using different \(\text{NN}_{\alpha_{h}^{\text{query}}}\), \(\text{NN}_{\alpha_{h}^{\text{key}}}\), \(\text{NN}_{\beta_{h}}\), where \(h\) indexes the different parameters. These are passed to \(f^{v}\) and concatenated:

\[f^{v}\left(\{\mathbf{\bar{e}}_{i}^{\prime h}\}_{h=1\ldots N^{h}}\right)=\text {NN}_{v}\left([\mathbf{\bar{e}}_{i}^{\prime 1},\ldots,\mathbf{\bar{e}}_{i}^{ \prime N_{h}}]\right)\]

Vertex Attention Interaction Networks (Hoshen, 2017) are very similar to single-headed SA, but use Euclidean distance for the attentional similarity metric, with shared parameters across the attention inputs' embeddings, and also use the input node feature in the node update function,

\[\alpha^{e}\left(\mathbf{v}_{r_{k}},\mathbf{v}_{s_{k}}\right) =\exp\left(-\|\text{NN}_{\alpha}\left(\mathbf{v}_{r_{k}}\right)- \text{NN}_{\alpha}\left(\mathbf{v}_{s_{k}}\right)\|^{2}\right)\] \[\beta^{e}\left(\mathbf{v}_{s_{k}}\right) =\text{NN}_{\beta}\left(\mathbf{v}_{s_{k}}\right)\] \[\phi^{v}\left(\mathbf{\bar{e}}_{i}^{\prime},\mathbf{v}_{i}, \mathbf{u}\right)\coloneqq f^{v}\left(\mathbf{\bar{e}}_{i}^{\prime}\right) =\text{NN}_{v}\left([\mathbf{\bar{e}}_{i}^{\prime},\mathbf{v}_{i}]\right)\]

Graph Attention Networks (Velickovic et al., 2018) are also similar to multi-headed SA, but use a neural network as the attentional similarity metric, with shared parameters across the attention inputs' embeddings:

\[\alpha^{e}\left(\mathbf{v}_{r_{k}},\mathbf{v}_{s_{k}}\right) =\exp\left(\text{NN}_{\alpha^{\prime}}\left(\left[\text{NN}_{ \alpha}\left(\mathbf{v}_{r_{k}}\right),\text{NN}_{\alpha}\left(\mathbf{v}_{s_{ k}}\right)\right)\right)\] \[\beta^{e}\left(\mathbf{v}_{s_{k}}\right) =\text{NN}_{\beta}\left(\mathbf{v}_{s_{k}}\right)\] \[\phi^{v}\left(\mathbf{\bar{e}}_{i}^{\prime},\mathbf{v}_{i}, \mathbf{u}\right)\coloneqq f^{v}\left(\{\mathbf{\bar{e}}_{i}^{\prime h}\}_{h=1 \ldots N_{h}}\right) =\text{NN}_{v}\left([\mathbf{\bar{e}}_{i}^{\prime 1},\ldots,\mathbf{\bar{e}}_{i}^{ \prime N_{h}}]\right)\]

Stretching beyond the specific non-local formulation, Shaw et al. (2018) extended multi-headed SA with relative position encodings. "Relative" refers to an encoding of the spatial distance between nodes in a sequence or other signal in a metric space. This can be expressed in GN language as an edge attribute \(\mathbf{e}_{k}\), and replacing the \(\beta^{e}\left(\mathbf{v}_{s_{k}}\right)\) from multi-headed SA above with:

\[\beta^{e}\left(\mathbf{e}_{k},\mathbf{v}_{s_{k}}\right)=\text{NN}_{e}\left( \mathbf{v}_{s_{k}}\right)+\mathbf{e}_{k}\]

#### Belief Propagation embeddings

Finally, we briefly summarize how the general "structure2vec" algorithm of Dai et al. (2016) can fit into our framework. In order to do so, we need to slightly modify our main Equation 1, i.e.:

\[\boldsymbol{\bar{\epsilon}}_{k} =\rho\left(\left\{\mathbf{e}_{l}\right\}_{\begin{subarray}{c}s_{l =r_{k}}\\ r_{l}\neq s_{k}\end{subarray}}\right)\coloneqq\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}r_{l}=s _{k}\\ s_{l}\neq r_{k}\end{subarray}}\mathbf{e}_{l}\] \[\mathbf{e}^{\prime}_{k} =\phi^{e}\left(\boldsymbol{\bar{\epsilon}}_{k}\right) \coloneqq f(\boldsymbol{\bar{\epsilon}}_{k})=\mathrm{NN}( \boldsymbol{\bar{\epsilon}}_{k})\] \[\boldsymbol{\bar{\mathrm{e}}}^{\prime}_{i} =\rho\left(\left\{\mathbf{e}^{\prime}_{k}\right\}_{r_{k}=i}\right) \coloneqq\sum_{\left\{k:\,r_{k}=i\right\}}\mathbf{e}_{k}\] \[\mathbf{v}^{\prime}_{i} =\phi^{v}\left(\boldsymbol{\bar{\mathrm{e}}}^{\prime}_{i}\right) \coloneqq f(\boldsymbol{\bar{\mathrm{e}}}^{\prime}_{i})=\mathrm{ NN}(\boldsymbol{\bar{\mathrm{e}}}^{\prime}_{i})\]

Edges' features now takes the meaning of "message" between their receiver and sender; note that there is only one set of parameters to learn for both the edges and nodes updates.